@page "/revise"
@inject IDraftStateService DraftStateService
@inject NavigationManager NavigationManager

@*
    The walk: the domain's agents, one screen each, read rather than interviewed.

    Nothing here calls a model and nothing here writes to the Draft, and both are the point. Somebody
    who imported a domain to correct something usually does not know where it is — they want to leaf
    through until they recognise it, the way a track is found by pressing next rather than by knowing
    its number. Leafing has to cost what leafing costs: a screen, instantly. A step that composed a
    question on the way past would charge a Performance call and a wait for a step nobody stopped at,
    which breaks the one gesture this page exists for.

    So the agent is shown whole — its five sections as they stand — for reading, and reading only:
    none of the five is its own way in any more. A fix to one section can leave another wrong (a
    Target that now promises something no tool of it backs is the ordinary way a domain drifts), and
    a client who could open exactly the section they meant to fix would have no reason to ever look
    at the rest. So there is one way in per agent, and it always opens at Target: the interview it
    hands over to walks every section from there, forcing the model to recheck what the change moved
    rather than trusting the other four are still true. The two skips beside the header are for the
    case where the point is on another agent. The model, the session and the agent leaving the
    configuration all wait until editing is actually asked for: an agent read on the way past is
    never taken out of the domain, so closing the tab in the middle of a walk loses nothing at all.

    This is the only place the walk exists. The interview it hands over to is the interview, unchanged
    — an agent that already exists is walked by the same five steps, asked the same way, as one being
    written now; only the mode each reads in differs.
*@

<div class="alembic-page alembic-wide">

    @if (Agents.Count == 0)
    {
        <div class="panel">
            <h2>Nothing to go over</h2>
            <p class="muted">
                This domain holds no agent yet. Bring a configuration, or distil one.
            </p>
        </div>
    }
    else if (Current is { } agent)
    {
        <div class="walk">

            @*
                Which of how many, and the agents either side by name. A bare "3 of 7" says where the
                client is but not what is next, and what is next is exactly what they are deciding
                when they reach for the skip.

                On the first agent there is nothing behind, so the skip is not drawn at all rather
                than drawn disabled — a control that can never do anything is not a quieter version
                of itself, it is a button with nothing behind it. An empty spacer of the same size
                takes its place instead of removing it outright, so the name does not lurch to the
                edge the one time in the walk it has no opposite number.
            *@
            <div class="walk-head">
                @if (At > 0)
                {
                    <button class="walk-step" @onclick="Previous" title="The agent before this one">
                        &#9198; @Named(Agents[At - 1])
                    </button>
                }
                else
                {
                    <span class="walk-step walk-step-spacer" aria-hidden="true"></span>
                }

                <div class="walk-who">
                    <h1>@Named(agent)</h1>
                    <span class="walk-count">@(At + 1) of @Agents.Count</span>
                </div>

                @*
                    Past the last agent is the finished domain, which is where an interview ends when
                    its map is spent. A walk that looped would have no way of saying it was over, and
                    reviewing what you have just corrected is the thing you came to do next.
                *@
                <button class="walk-step" @onclick="Next">
                    @(Last ? "Review the domain" : Named(Agents[At + 1])) &#9197;
                </button>
            </div>

            @if (Description(agent) is { Length: > 0 } description)
            {
                <p class="walk-when">Comes when someone asks about @Lower(description)</p>
            }

            @*
                Five rows, each the whole of what is written, for reading only. Not clipped: a screen
                opened to read what an agent says, that then shows four fifths of a sentence and an
                ellipsis, has answered nothing — the part that got cut is the part they came for.

                The labels are the rail's own, matching the interview's own vocabulary, but none of
                them is pressable any more: editing is one gesture for the whole agent, below, not
                five doors into five separate promises about what a fix will and will not touch.
            *@
            <div class="walk-sections">
                @foreach (Section part in Sections(agent))
                {
                    <div class="walk-section @(part.Written is null ? "unwritten" : null)">
                        <span class="walk-label">@part.Label</span>

                        @if (part.Label == "Toolkit" && agent.Tools.Count > 0)
                        {
                            <div class="walk-value tools">
                                @foreach (ToolDraft tool in agent.Tools)
                                {
                                    <div class="tool-line">&bull; <strong>@AgentRows.Signature(tool)</strong>@(string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(tool.Description) ? null : $" — {tool.Description}")</div>
                                }
                            </div>
                        }
                        else
                        {
                            <span class="walk-value">
                                @(part.Written ?? "Not written yet")
                            </span>
                        }
                    </div>
                }
            </div>

            @*
                One way in, always the same door: the Target. Editing hands the whole agent to the
                interview and lets it walk every section from there, so a change here is checked
                against what comes after it instead of trusted to leave the rest alone.
            *@
            <button class="primary walk-edit" @onclick="() => Edit(agent)">
                Edit this agent
            </button>

        </div>
    }

    <Back />

</div>

@code {
    /// <summary>
    /// One section of the agent on screen: what it is called, and what it says.
    /// </summary>
    private sealed record Section(string Label, string? Written);

    /// <summary>
    /// Which agent of the domain is on screen.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// In the address rather than in the circuit, so a walk survives the reload a Blazor Server
    /// circuit does not — and so Import's own agent cards can hand somebody straight to the one they
    /// were reading, instead of to the first and a row of presses.
    /// </remarks>
    [SupplyParameterFromQuery(Name = "at")]
    public int? Where { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// The agents of the domain, in the domain's own order.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// The fallback has no agent and is not one of these: it is where the classifier sends what it
    /// cannot place, it belongs to every domain, and there is nothing about it for anyone to correct.
    /// </remarks>
    private IReadOnlyList<AgentDraft> Agents =>
        DraftStateService.Current?.Agents ?? [];

    /// <summary>
    /// Where the walk stands, held to the domain it is walking so an address typed by hand or an
    /// agent removed since cannot put it past the end.
    /// </summary>
    private int At => Math.Clamp(Where ?? 0, 0, Math.Max(0, Agents.Count - 1));

    private AgentDraft? Current => Agents.Count > 0 ? Agents[At] : null;

    private bool Last => At + 1 >= Agents.Count;

    /// <summary>
    /// The intent that reaches an agent, which is where its name and its description live.
    /// </summary>
    private IntentDraft? Intent(AgentDraft agent) =>
        DraftStateService.Current?.Intents.FirstOrDefault(i =>
            string.Equals(i.Name, agent.ID, StringComparison.OrdinalIgnoreCase));

    /// <summary>
    /// The client's own word for an agent: its button, which is what they called it.
    /// </summary>
    private string Named(AgentDraft agent) =>
        Intent(agent)?.Label ?? agent.ID ?? "an agent";

    private string? Description(AgentDraft agent) => Intent(agent)?.Description;

    /// <summary>
    /// Lowers a description's first letter so it reads on from "asks about".
    /// </summary>
    private static string Lower(string description) =>
        char.ToLowerInvariant(description[0]) + description[1..];

    /// <summary>
    /// The agent as five sections, in the order the interview writes them.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// The Toolkit is named by its tools rather than by prose, because that is what it is — a list of
    /// what this agent can reach. <see cref="AgentRows.Toolkit"/> only says whether it is empty; the
    /// markup renders it one tool per line, signature bolded, straight off <c>agent.Tools</c> — the
    /// same way the panel behind the map's own tag does, so a client leafing here and one still
    /// mid-interview meet the same toolkit written the same way.
    /// </remarks>
    private static IReadOnlyList<Section> Sections(AgentDraft agent) =>
    [
        new Section("Target", AgentRows.Plain(agent.Target)),
        new Section("Personality", AgentRows.Plain(agent.Personality)),
        new Section("Toolkit", AgentRows.Toolkit(agent.Tools)),
        new Section("Instructions", AgentRows.Plain(agent.Instructions)),
        new Section("Formatting", AgentRows.Plain(agent.Formatting))
    ];

    private void Previous() => Go(At - 1);

    /// <summary>
    /// On past this agent, or out of the walk when it was the last.
    /// </summary>
    private void Next()
    {
        if (Last)
            NavigationManager.NavigateTo("review");
        else
            Go(At + 1);
    }

    private void Go(int at) => NavigationManager.NavigateTo($"revise?at={at}");

    /// <summary>
    /// Hands the whole agent over to the interview, which is where the model finally comes on. It
    /// always opens at the Target — see the doctrine at the top of this file for why there is no
    /// longer a way to name a section here.
    /// </summary>
    private void Edit(AgentDraft agent) =>
        NavigationManager.NavigateTo(
            $"interview?revise={Uri.EscapeDataString(agent.ID ?? string.Empty)}&back={At}");
}
